// Detection: Suspicious Mailbox Auto-Forwarding Rule Created
// MITRE ATT&CK: T1114.003 (Email Collection: Email Forwarding Rule) —
//   commonly follows T1566 (Phishing) as a post-compromise persistence step
// Log source: Microsoft Sentinel / Defender for Office 365 — OfficeActivity
//
// What it detects:
//   New inbox rules that forward or redirect mail to an external
//   domain, especially rules with generic hide-the-evidence names
//   (e.g. auto-deleting or moving the forwarded copy to an unused
//   folder) — a classic move after a successful phishing/BEC
//   compromise to maintain visibility into a mailbox after the
//   password is changed.
//
// Why it matters:
//   Forwarding rules survive password resets and MFA re-enrollment,
//   making them a durable foothold for business email compromise. This
//   is one of the highest-value, lowest-volume detections for
//   protecting executive and finance mailboxes specifically.
//
// False positive considerations:
//   - Legitimate use cases exist (assistants forwarding to a shared
//     mailbox, employees forwarding to a personal address with prior
//     approval) — cross-reference against an approved-forwarding
//     exception list rather than blanket-blocking.
//   - Vacation/out-of-office auto-forward setups by end users are
//     common and usually benign; the external-domain + rule-name
//     heuristics below help separate these from BEC persistence, but
//     analyst review is still recommended for every hit.

OfficeActivity
| where TimeGenerated > ago(1d)
| where Operation in ("New-InboxRule", "Set-InboxRule")
| extend Parameters = parse_json(Parameters)
| extend RuleName = tostring(Parameters[0].Value)
| extend ForwardTo = tostring(Parameters[1].Value)
| where isnotempty(ForwardTo)
| where ForwardTo !has "yourcompany.com" // replace with your own accepted domain(s)
| project TimeGenerated, UserId, Operation, RuleName, ForwardTo, ClientIP
| order by TimeGenerated desc
